.TH libtree 1 "2020-04-13" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
libtree \- inspect shared object dependencies as a tree or ldd-style mappings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B libtree 
[
.I option
]... [--] [
.I file
]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libtree
prints the shared libraries required by each program or shared library on the command line as a tree. By default certain common system libraries are hidden to prune the tree.
ELF files are inspected without executing the target or invoking its loader.
The ldd format includes common libraries and their transitive dependencies.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP "-h, --help"
Print usage
.IP "--version"
Print version info
.IP "--format tree|ldd, --format=tree|ldd"
Select the output format. The default is tree.
The ldd format prints tab-indented mappings using the requested
.B DT_NEEDED
name and resolved path, or "NAME => not found" for unresolved dependencies.
Each resolved file is printed once per input, identified by device and inode;
each unresolved name is printed once per input.
The ELF interpreter from
.B PT_INTERP
is printed as a standalone path, without a duplicate dependency mapping.
Inputs are resolved independently. FILE: headings are printed only when
multiple inputs are supplied.
Inputs with neither an interpreter nor dependencies print "statically linked",
including static PIE and dependency-free shared objects, regardless of whether
they contain
.BR PT_DYNAMIC .
Missing dependencies retain libtree's nonzero exit status and stderr diagnostics.
.IP "-p, --path"
Show the path of libraries instead of their 
.B soname
This option is redundant in ldd mode.
.IP "-v"
Show libraries skipped by default
Verbosity flags affect only tree output.
.IP "-vv"
Show dependencies of libraries skipped by default
.IP "-vvv"
Show dependencies of already encountered libraries
.IP "--ldconf arg"
Path to custom
.I ld.so.conf
or
.I ld-elf.so.conf
file
.IP "--sysroot dir, --rootfs dir"
Treat
.I dir
as the root of the target file system, e.g. a cross-architecture rootfs.
Absolute paths and include patterns in ld.so.conf are resolved inside
.IR dir .
The default search paths, absolute
.B DT_RPATH
/
.B DT_RUNPATH
entries, absolute
.B DT_NEEDED
entries and the ldd interpreter lookup are resolved inside
.IR dir .
Paths derived from $ORIGIN and
.B LD_LIBRARY_PATH
are used as given. Unless --ldconf is passed, the config file is read from
.IR dir /etc/ld.so.conf.
.IP "--max-depth n"
Limit library traversal to a depth of at most
.IR n .
The value cannot be larger than 32.
This truncates dependency traversal in both formats: depth 0 omits dependencies,
and depth 1 includes only direct dependencies. In ldd mode, interpreter metadata
is shown regardless of this limit.
.IP "--"
All arguments after '--' are interpreted as paths, not flags.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
libtree --format tree /usr/bin/tar
libtree --format=ldd /usr/bin/tar
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
The ldd format provides static dependency mappings, not full loader emulation.
It retains libtree's search precedence and architecture checks, but loader cache
behavior, preloads, and hardware-capability selection can differ from runtime
resolution. Load addresses and virtual dependencies such as linux-vdso are
omitted, as are colors, tree branches, and search-path annotations.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B LD_LIBRARY_PATH
can be used to provide additional search paths.
.SH AUTHOR
Harmen Stoppels <me@harmenstoppels.nl>
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ldd (1)
